meta {
  name: D2 - Archive Issue
  type: graphql
  seq: 4
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}
  body: graphql
  auth: inherit
}

body:graphql {
  mutation ArchiveIssue($issueId: String!) {
    issueArchive(id: $issueId) {
      success
    }
  }
}

body:graphql:vars {
  {
    "issueId": "431c9f95-fe23-44cb-9391-f4880c51bede"
  }
}

docs {
  Reference: https://linear.app/developers/graphql

  **Only the user runs this. Never the assistant.** This is a state-mutating call.

  Archives exactly ONE issue (`EST-2`, "Connect your tools"). Reversible — Linear's UI can
  unarchive it, and `issueUnarchive` exists if you would rather undo it via the API.

  Closes THE single highest-risk unverified assumption in the whole cluster.

  `14 - Issues Include Archived` established that Linear hides archived rows by default and
  that `archivedAt` is exposed on all four types, so the plan sets `includeArchived: true`
  everywhere and treats `archivedAt != null` as the tombstone. But with zero archived
  records in the workspace, two things remain pure assumption:

  1. That archived rows actually DO appear when `includeArchived: true` is passed.
  2. **That archiving BUMPS `updatedAt`.** This is the load-bearing one. If archiving
     leaves `updatedAt` unchanged, then an `updatedAt`-cursored incremental sweep will
     never re-emit the row, the archival never reaches the destination, and downstream
     keeps a live-looking copy of a deleted record indefinitely. `includeArchived: true`
     would not save us — only a periodic full backfill would. If this turns out to be the
     case, the cluster needs a scheduled full re-sweep and the plan must change.

  **Re-run after:** `14 - Issues Include Archived` — check that (a) `withArchived` now
  returns 4 rows while `liveOnly` returns 3, and (b) the archived row's `updatedAt` is
  NEWER than its pre-archive value, not the original bulk-seed timestamp.

  Please report the archived issue's `updatedAt` and `archivedAt` from the re-run.
}
